  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule states \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", fails to distinguish a reply whose only code fence uses a non-JSON language tag from a reply with no fence and the same trailing top-level object. Reply A is the text ` ```python\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n```\\n{\\\"b\\\": 2}`. Reply B is the text `{\\\"b\\\": 2}`. The rule requires Reply A to score {\\\"a\\\": 1}, because a code fence holds an object and the last object inside any fence is {\\\"a\\\": 1}; it requires Reply B to score {\\\"b\\\": 2}, because no fence holds an object and the last top-level object is {\\\"b\\\": 2}. The check, however, returns {\\\"b\\\": 2} for both: its _FENCE regex only matches ```json, ```JSON, or a bare ```, so the ```python fence is not recognized, and the top-level pool's last object is {\\\"b\\\": 2}. I would be wrong if recover_json_object on Reply A returns {\\\"a\\\": 1} (i.e., if the check recognizes the ```python fence), or if the documented rule is read as covering only fences with json, JSON, or no language tag."
